he Ch&#39;ing Period/T&#39;u-hai\">T'u-hai</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367783\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367783\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3658537</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 2 \u2014 Tu Y\u00fceh</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">J. C. Yang</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"780\" data-page-number=\"780\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/178\" data-page-index=\"178\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/178\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;178\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>TU</b> Y\u00fceh \u675c\u8d8a (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u541b\u7570,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u7d2b\u5cef, \u6587\u5b9a\u5148\u751f), Dec. 12, 1596\u20131682, Jan. 4, scholar and calligrapher, was born in the village of Tung-chiang, a district of Ting-hsing, Hopei. His father, Tu Chien \u675c\u9451 (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u8861\u5b87), was a military <i>ch\u00fc-j\u00ean</i> of 1609. Tu Y\u00fceh studied under his townsman, Lu Shan-chi (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Sun_Ch%27i-f%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Sun Ch&#39;i-f\u00eang\">Sun Ch'i-f\u00eang</a></span>). As a student he was distinguished for his genius and was given by his teacher the appellation Ch\u00fcn-i\n\u541b\u7570, \"exceptional person.\" During the imprisonment of Wei Ta-chung, Tso Kuang-tou (for both see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Yang_Lien\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yang Lien\">Yang Lien</a></span>) and Chou Shun-ch'ang \u5468\u9806\u660c (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u666f\u6587,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u84fc\u6d32,&#32;1584\u20131626)\u2014who were held on false charges by <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wei_Chung-hsien\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wei Chung-hsien\">Wei Chung-hsien</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]\u2014Tu and his life-long friend, Sun Ch'i-f\u00eang, collected money among friends in order to save their lives. At the risk of his own life he hid Wei Ta-chung's son, Wei Hs\u00fceh-i \u9b4f\u5b78\u4f0a (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u5b50\u656c), and Chou Shun-ch'ang's close friend, Chu Tsu-w\u00ean \u6731\u7956\u6587 (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u5b8c\u592b,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u4e09\u5fa9\u5c45\u58eb), in his home, until the danger was past. This act of courage brought him nation-wide fame. After the change of dynasty, he lived in seclusion and taught in the neighboring district of Hsin-an. He was recommended to take the <i>po-hs\u00fceh hung-tz'\u016d</i> examination of 1679, but on his way to Peking he met <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Fu_Shan\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Fu Shan\">Fu Shan</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] and like the latter declined to participate in the examination, giving as his reason excessive age. He was permitted to return home with the honorary title of Secretary of the Grand Secretariat. His collected works, entitled \u7d2b\u5cef\u96c6 <i>Tz\u016d-f\u00eang chi</i>, in 14 <i>ch\u00fcan</i>, containing his poems and essays, were compiled by his student, Yang Chan \u694a\u6e5b.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[<i>Ting-hsing hsien-chih</i> (1799) 8/33b, (1890) 11/21a; 2/66/4a; 3/125/5a; 7/48/5b; 10/10/10a; 15/8/15b; 17/1/4a; 30/3/6b; 32/4/7b.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">J. C. Yang</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Fu Shan"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Sun Ch'i-f\u00eang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/T'u-hai"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Tu Shou-t'ien"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wei Chung-hsien"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yang Lien"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:J. C. Yang"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 2\n | onlysection = tu yueh\n | previous = Tu Shou-t'ien\n | next     = T'u-hai\n | from     = 178\n | to       = 178\n | notes    =\n | section_author = J. C. Yang\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Tu Y\u00fceh}}"}}}